  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 88,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a real Site A speed advantage across order size, product mix, shift, and staffing pressure, generally about 5%–9%, with the largest gap on complex orders. The new scanner contributes causally to faster picking: randomized crossover evidence indicates an average reduction in individual picking time of about 4% (95% CI: 1%–7%). However, it is not supported as the sole or clearly primary driver of the full site-level difference. Longer worker tenure and more stable handovers at Site A are retained as meaningful competing explanations, together accounting for roughly 2 percentage points of the estimated advantage. Order complexity or interactions with operating practices should also be retained because the gap increases on high-complexity orders. Explanations based solely on order size, product mix, shift, staffing pressure, unreliable task classification, or ordinary within-site timing error should be rejected as sufficient accounts. The reproducible cross-site timestamp configuration offset must be corrected or explicitly modeled; until then, precise attribution of the remaining site advantage is constrained."
